-- title:
--   Theorem 3.1 — value approximation is in TFNP and the gap problem in NP ∩ coNP
-- statement:
--   For limit-average and reachability objectives in concurrent stochastic games:
--   1. the CsgApproxValue problem is in TFNP;
--   2. the CsgGapValue problem is in NP $\cap$ coNP.
--
--   Here CsgApproxValue asks, given a game with rational transition probabilities, a target set $T$ or a rational reward function $r:S\times A\times B\to[0,1]$, and $\varepsilon=2^{-B_\varepsilon}$ in binary, for $\hat v:S\to\mathbb Q$ with
--   $$|\hat v(s)-\mathrm{val}_\varphi(s)|\le\varepsilon\quad\text{for every state }s.$$
--   CsgGapValue additionally takes a state $s$ and a rational threshold $\alpha$, and distinguishes $\mathrm{val}_\varphi(s)\ge\alpha+\varepsilon$ from $\mathrm{val}_\varphi(s)\le\alpha-\varepsilon$, under the promise that one of the two holds.
--
--   Item 1 means that CsgApproxValue reduces in polynomial time to a total search problem with polynomially bounded answers checked in polynomial time. Item 2 is membership of the promise problem in promise-NP and in promise-coNP. Both clauses are stated for both objectives.
--
--   This improves the previous TFNP[NP] upper bound to TFNP. Values are defined over all history-dependent randomized strategies, and the limit-average payoff is the lim-inf average.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: a finite game G = (S, A, B, δ) with |S| = n and at most m actions per player → states Fin n, actions Fin mA and Fin mB with n, mA, mB ≥ 1, m = max(mA, mB), distributions as nonnegative real vectors summing to one; reason: relabelling of finite sets; nonempty state and action sets are the source game convention
--   - encoding: the probability measure on valid plays Ω_s generated by finite histories, strategies on valid histories → the Ionescu-Tulcea trajectory measure on all sequences in (S × A × B)^ℕ; strategies are defined on all finite stage lists; reason: invalid plays have probability zero and values of strategies on invalid histories never matter, so every expectation and value is unchanged
--   - encoding: input: rational probabilities and rewards; ε = 2^{-Bε} given in binary with Bε + 1 bits; a succinct discount factor λ = 1 − 2^{-2^{Bλ}} → self-delimiting binary codes of n, |A|, |B| and all rationals; ε encoded as the binary natural 2^{Bε}; λ encoded as the binary natural 2^{Bλ}; a state as a binary natural; a target set as its characteristic vector; Bε ≥ 1 in valid instances; reason: these are binary encodings of exactly the source's inputs, with lengths linear in Bε and Bλ as the source specifies; malformed strings are not valid instances (outside the problem)
--   - encoding: CsgApproxValue is in TFNP (the paper, printed p. 17: it reduces in polynomial time to the TFNP problem of finding a witness) → there is a search problem R with InTFNP R (total on valid inputs, uniformly polynomially bounded answers, polynomial-time checkable pairs on Cook machines) and a polynomial-time search reduction (instance map, answer map correct for every answer of R) from CsgApproxValue to R; reason: this is the source's own meaning of membership of a search problem whose answer relation is not known to be checkable; the published InTFNP and SearchReduces definitions are used unchanged
--   - encoding: CsgGapValue is in NP ∩ coNP under the promise that val(s) ∉ (α − ε, α + ε) → promise-NP and promise-coNP: a polynomial-time checking relation with polynomial certificates for every promised yes-instance (resp. no-instance) and no certificate for any promised no-instance (resp. yes-instance); strings outside the promise are unconstrained; reason: the standard promise-class reading of the source's promise statement; Bool certificates are without loss of generality
--   - generalization: the threshold α of CsgGapValue is a rational defined using at most B_inp bits → α is an arbitrary rational, encoded in binary as part of the input; reason: the promise problems with arbitrary α contain the source's as a sub-promise (every source yes/no instance is a yes/no instance here), so promise-NP / promise-coNP membership of the Lean problem implies the source's; the bit-length of α is part of the input length
-- source:
--   Ali Asadi, Krishnendu Chatterjee, The Computational Complexity of Concurrent Stochastic Games: Exact Value and Approximation, STOC 2027 submission #285 (October 2026), SHA-256 5cff187cf6b230b3bdfe540f2832f824d1ddb6acb43239fbf469d37c43b0d461, https://github.com/sigact-stoc/stoc2027/releases/download/papers-1/285.pdf, Theorem 3.1, printed p. 6 (PDF p. 8); proof printed pp. 31–32 (PDF pp. 33–34)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ConcurrentStochasticApprox_Approx_Problems
import Definitions.Def_NetworkEquilibriumCLS_LinearNash_Encoding
import Definitions.Def_NetworkEquilibriumCLS_LinearNash_Complexity
open Finset NetworkEquilibriumCLS.LinearNash

namespace ConcurrentStochasticApprox.Approx

/-- **Theorem 3.1 (Value approximation).** For limit-average and reachability objectives,
(i) CsgApproxValue is in TFNP: it reduces in polynomial time to a total search problem with
polynomially bounded, polynomial-time checkable answers; and (ii) CsgGapValue is in NP ∩ coNP (as a
promise problem). -/
theorem theorem_3_1 :
    ((∃ R : SearchProblem, InTFNP R ∧ SearchReduces limAvgApproxSearch R) ∧
      PromiseNP limAvgGap ∧ PromiseCoNP limAvgGap) ∧
    ((∃ R : SearchProblem, InTFNP R ∧ SearchReduces reachApproxSearch R) ∧
      PromiseNP reachGap ∧ PromiseCoNP reachGap) := by sorry

end ConcurrentStochasticApprox.Approx
